  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "\\(f(x)=x^2\\), where \\(x\\in[-1,2]\\). What is the maximum value?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "4",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The function values at the endpoints \\(-1\\) and \\(2\\) are \\(1\\) and \\(4\\), and the value at the vertex \\(x=0\\) is \\(0\\). The maximum is \\(4\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Prove an upper bound\n\nFor \\(-1\\le x\\le2\\), both \\(2-x\\) and \\(2+x\\) are nonnegative. Their product lets us compare \\(x^2\\) directly with \\(4\\):\n\\[\n4-x^2=(2-x)(2+x)\\ge0.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(f(x)\\le4\\) throughout the interval. Equality holds at the allowed endpoint \\(x=2\\), so the maximum value is \\(4\\).",
